    "perplexity": {
      "description": "Grand Parade\" by The Frank and Walters is the iconic 1997 follow-up to their debut, Trains, Boats and Planes, cementing the Cork trio's status as one of Ireland’s most popular and influential alternative pop bands. Breaking a five-year silence, the album emerged as a singular pop document that is both heartfelt and anthemic, retaining trace elements of the quirkiness that made the band a sensation in 1992 while delivering an infectiously upbeat collection of criminally underrated guitar pop . Featuring timeless indie classics such as \"Colours,\" \"Indian Ocean,\" \"How Can I Exist?,\" and \"Tony Cochrane,\" the album cost a blockbuster £250,000 to record and is widely regarded as one of the greatest Irish albums of the decade .\n\nThe album is now being celebrated with a special 30th-anniversary reissue, marking its first-ever release on vinyl, supported by a tour and a celebratory show at the Cork Opera House . Teaming up with the renowned Scottish label Last Night From Glasgow (noted as \"Past Night From Glasgow\" in the prompt context), the reissue was released on June 30, 2026, in three distinct vinyl colors: Tangerine, Bluebell, and Apple Green . This reissue showcases the band at their best, highlighting the album's enduring legacy and its status as a seminal work in the rock and indie genres .\n\nCritically, \"Grand Parade\" has been praised for its guitar pop sensibilities, with NME describing it as a standout record, and it remains a fan favorite for tracks like \"Colours\" and \"Tony Cochrane\" . The album's 30th-year celebration underscores its significance in Irish music history, bridging the gap between the band's early UK chart success and their later, more refined pop sound . As a reissue of the original 1997 album, this release offers a fresh opportunity for listeners to experience the timeless energy and ambition that defined The Frank and Walters' second chapter .",
